        %h2 Concepts
        %dl.grid
          %dt Ferocity
          %dd Stroke/return asymmetry and dwell — 0 is a pure sine.
          %dt Waveform
          %dd The wingbeat curve; amplitude comes from deg/s ÷ 2·freq.
          %dt Glide angle
          %dd Wing centre offset for gliding flight.
          %dt Flapping centre offset
          %dd Centre bias during the flap stroke (−15…+15 deg).
          %dt Rudder mixing
          %dd Yaw/roll blend that drives the crest rudder.
          %dt Throttle ferocity mix
          %dd How much throttle couples into ferocity.
          %dt Failsafe
          %dd Wings return to neutral if the link drops.

        %section#ferocity-waveform.waveform-docs
          %h2 Ferocity waveform geometry
          %p Ferocity controls both the character and the timing of a half-stroke. Internally, the WebUI’s 0–100 setting maps to 0–8. Higher downstroke ferocity shortens the downstroke’s share of the cycle; higher return ferocity shortens the return share. The remaining time is transferred to the opposite half—there is no pause at zero.
          %p The per-profile Ferocity Shape control selects the high-ferocity character. At 0% it favours a finite plateau/square shape. At 100% it favours a rounded pyramidal path. Each half still uses its own ferocity, so a low-ferocity half remains elongated and sinusoidal while a high-ferocity half approaches a direct diagonal traversal.

          .waveform-explorer{"data-ferocity-explorer": true}
            .waveform-controls
              %label{for: "wave-down-ferocity"}
                %span Down ferocity
                %output{"data-value": "down", for: "wave-down-ferocity"} 8.0
                %input#wave-down-ferocity{"data-control": "down", type: "range", min: "0", max: "8", step: "0.1", value: "8"}
              %label{for: "wave-up-ferocity"}
                %span Up ferocity
                %output{"data-value": "up", for: "wave-up-ferocity"} 0.0
                %input#wave-up-ferocity{"data-control": "up", type: "range", min: "0", max: "8", step: "0.1", value: "0"}
              %label{for: "wave-shape-mix"}
                %span Shape mix
                %output{"data-value": "mix", for: "wave-shape-mix"} 100%
                %input#wave-shape-mix{"data-control": "mix", type: "range", min: "0", max: "100", step: "1", value: "100"}

            .waveform-presets{"aria-label": "Waveform examples"}
              %button{type: "button", "data-preset": "8,0,100"} Extreme 8 / 0
              %button{type: "button", "data-preset": "7,1,100"} Near-extreme 7 / 1
              %button{type: "button", "data-preset": "8,6,100"} High base 8 / 6
              %button{type: "button", "data-preset": "8,6,50"} High base, mixed

            %p.waveform-metric{"data-waveform-metric": true, "aria-live": "polite"}

            .waveform-chart
              %h3 Whole cycle
              .waveform-legend
                %span
                  %i.current
                  Current waveform
                %span
                  %i.direct
                  Exact direct reference
                %span
                  %i.boundary
                  Reversal
              %svg{"data-chart": "cycle", role: "img", "aria-label": "Wing position over the complete beat cycle"}

            .waveform-chart
              %h3 Each half stretched to equal width
              .waveform-legend
                %span
                  %i.down
                  Down half
                %span
                  %i.up
                  Up half, direction aligned
                %span
                  %i.direct
                  Exact diagonal
              %svg{"data-chart": "halves", role: "img", "aria-label": "Normalized downstroke and upstroke shapes"}

          %h3 Reading extreme combinations
          %p A 7 / 1 combination clearly produces a short pointed downstroke and a long sinusoidal return. Exact and near-maximum values require care: the current duration law uses the amount remaining below ferocity 8. Consequently, 8 / 0 allocates only about 0.125% of the cycle to the downstroke, while even 8 / 6 allocates about 0.5%. Position remains continuous, but the requested speed and acceleration can become physically unreachable.
          %aside.waveform-caution
            %strong Bench-test before flight.
            The explorer describes the commanded mathematical waveform, not guaranteed servo motion. Avoid extreme asymmetric settings until amplitude, frequency, linkage geometry, and servo-speed limits have been verified without aerodynamic load.
